(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When is catheter ablation used for premature atrial contractions?","choices":["When they trigger fibrillation","Whenever any are found","When they cause no symptoms","When they come with sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation is used if premature atrial contractions are frequent or are triggering atrial fibrillation; otherwise a beta blocker or reassurance is used. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which cardiac event is a listed cause of junctional rhythm?","choices":["Hypovolemic shock","Untreated hyperthyroidism","Inferior myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause of junctional rhythm, with sinus node dysfunction, the acute period after cardiac surgery and digoxin toxicity. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia depends on which structure?","choices":["A damaged sinus node","An accessory pathway","A circuit inside the node","Three atrial foci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is seen in patients with an accessory pathway, such as Wolff-Parkinson-White; the loop uses the atrioventricular node and the pathway. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which atrioventricular conduction ratio is most common in atrial flutter?","choices":["2:1","1:1","3:1","4:1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When the atrioventricular node blocks one of every two atrial impulses, the result is 2:1 block, the most common pattern in atrial flutter. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"In sinus arrhythmia, how does the heart rate change with breathing?","choices":["Unchanged; only the P-wave shape varies","Faster on inspiration, slower on expiration","Slower on inspiration, faster on expiration","Faster in both phases of the breath"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus arrhythmia is irregular with a pattern: the sinus rate speeds up with inspiration and slows with expiration, while every beat keeps a normal P wave and narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does age relate to the risk of atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Risk falls after age 65","Age plays no part","Older age raises the risk","Risk peaks in adolescence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is commonly seen in the elderly, alongside hypertension, diabetes, cardiac disease, family history, obesity, sleep apnea and excessive alcohol. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the prognostic meaning of multifocal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["A poor prognostic sign","A benign finding","A sign of fitness","A normal aging change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multifocal atrial tachycardia is a poor prognostic sign because it occurs in acutely ill patients with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or heart failure. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which drug is among the medication causes of sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Digoxin","Atropine","An anticholinergic","A beta-agonist inhaler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The medication causes of sick sinus syndrome are beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin, all of which slow the sinus node. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is sinus arrhythmia best characterized?","choices":["An indication for a pacemaker","A normal, physiologic variant","A sign of sinus node disease","A precursor of atrial fibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus arrhythmia is normal and physiologic: a rate of 60 to 100 that speeds with inspiration and slows with expiration, with a P wave for every QRS and nothing to treat. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Besides converting the rhythm, what can adenosine show in a supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["ST segment elevation","A prolonged QT interval","A bundle branch block","Hidden P waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine may convert supraventricular tachycardia to normal sinus rhythm, or at least slow the rate so you can see whether P waves are there. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Continuous atrial fibrillation lasting more than 7 days but less than one year is called what?","choices":["Persistent","Paroxysmal","Long-standing persistent","Permanent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent atrial fibrillation is continuous for more than 7 days but less than one year and needs treatment; beyond one year it is long-standing persistent. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia?","choices":["Amiodarone","A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atropine is first line for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is hemodynamically stable atrial flutter treated first?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Immediate cardioversion","Repeated adenosine","Beta or calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; an unstable patient gets synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is a delta wave?","choices":["A slurred upstroke of the QRS","A notched, wide P wave","An inverted T wave","A sawtooth baseline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The delta wave is a slurred upstroke of the QRS complex, seen with a short PR interval and a widened QRS in Wolff-Parkinson-White. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"On a rhythm strip, how does atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White differ from ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Regular sawtooth at 300","Irregular, rates under 100","Irregular, rates near 300","Regular, rates 150 to 200"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular tachycardia is regular at about 150 to 200, while atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White is irregular, with some rates approaching 300. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is more common in whom?","choices":["Men","People with lung disease","People with heart surgery","Women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia affects women far more than men, commonly without heart disease, spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine or alcohol. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In sick sinus syndrome, when is observation alone appropriate?","choices":["The fast rhythm is ventricular","Symptoms do not match the bradycardia","Pauses are causing fainting","Bradycardia causes dizziness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management confirms whether symptoms correlate with episodes of bradycardia; if they do not, observation is appropriate, and if the bradycardia is symptomatic, a permanent pacemaker is placed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which rhythm most often alternates with bradycardia in sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial flutter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which fast and slow rhythms alternate, the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which rhythm is listed among those that can exceed 200 beats per minute and cause collapse?","choices":["Physiologic sinus tachycardia","Pre-excited atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rates above 200 with syncope or collapse are seen in accessory pathway reentry, nodal reentry and atrial fibrillation with an accessory pathway (for example Wolff-Parkinson-White). — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the usual rate of a junctional rhythm?","choices":["100 to 160","250 to 300","40 to 60","60 to 100"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm is regular, usually at 40 to 60 beats per minute, with a narrow QRS and P waves that are inverted, buried or after the QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Sinus bradycardia is a sinus rhythm at what rate?","choices":["60 to 100 beats per minute","Over 100 beats per minute","40 to 60 with inverted P waves","Under 60 beats per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sinus bradycardia is a regular sinus rhythm under 60 beats per minute, with a uniform P wave for every QRS, a normal PR interval and a narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How is a junctional rhythm generally tolerated?","choices":["Poorly, with syncope","It degenerates to fibrillation","Always needs a pacemaker","Well tolerated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm is generally well tolerated; management depends on the cause, from no treatment in a healthy person to a pacemaker for block or sick sinus syndrome. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"If premature atrial contractions are frequent, what should be looked for?","choices":["An accessory pathway","Digoxin toxicity","A long QT interval","Structural heart disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If premature atrial contractions are frequent, look for underlying structural heart disease; in a diseased heart they may precede supraventricular tachycardia, fibrillation or flutter. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"In whom are the symptoms of supraventricular tachycardia more pronounced?","choices":["Women more than men","Elderly patients or heart disease","Young, healthy athletes","Patients with normal hearts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of supraventricular tachycardia, such as palpitations, dyspnea and chest discomfort, may be more pronounced in the elderly and in those with underlying structural heart disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"When does atrial fibrillation become a medical emergency?","choices":["Severe heart failure or infarction","Whenever the rate exceeds 100","Whenever it is newly found","Whenever palpitations are felt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medical emergencies from atrial fibrillation include severe heart failure (pulmonary edema, hypotension) and atrial fibrillation in the setting of an acute myocardial infarction. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"In a healthy person, a junctional rhythm often reflects what?","choices":["A hidden accessory pathway","An overactive sinus node","Atrial scarring","Increased vagal tone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Junctional rhythm can be seen in healthy people with increased vagal tone, such as well-conditioned athletes, and it is generally well tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"In a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which step comes before any drug?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion","A vagal maneuver","An echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A vagal maneuver is first line for stable supraventricular (narrow complex) tachycardia, before adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the management of an asymptomatic junctional rhythm in an otherwise healthy person?","choices":["No treatment","A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of junctional rhythm depends on the cause: no treatment if asymptomatic in an otherwise healthy person, a permanent pacemaker for complete or high-grade block or sick sinus syndrome. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Sinus tachycardia is defined by a sinus rate in which range?","choices":["From 60 to 100","From 250 to 300","Above 100, up to 160","Above 160"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sinus tachycardia is a regular sinus rhythm above 100 up to 160 beats per minute, with a P wave for every QRS; above 160 the P waves are lost and the rhythm is called supraventricular tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which intravenous drug treats atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White?","choices":["Magnesium","Procainamide","Adenosine","Atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White is treated with intravenous procainamide, never adenosine, because blocking the normal atrioventricular node leaves the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
